  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14165968/assets/eb292b77908dbb4590b6bbc49c452215edd8e6668e003e0984074a7ca22bf4ff.png)\n\nWhat is the range of the function $f(x),$ shown above?",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the smallest and largest $y$-values in the graph.\n\n- The smallest $y$-value is $0.$ This occurs when $x=2.$\n\n- There is no largest $y$-value.\n\nThe function covers all the $y$-values between $0$ and $\\infty ,$ including $0.$\n\nTherefore, the range is $0\\le y<\\infty ,$ which can also be written as  $0\\le f(x)<\\infty .$",
